course: everyday
lesson: 10
title: Reasoning about the body moving in the world
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Two simple ideas explain a lot of everyday moving: gravity always pulls straight down, and a moving body keeps moving until something stops it.
explain: You stay up when your centre of gravity is above your feet; on a slope you lean forward going up and keep your knees bent and your steps small going down, so your weight stays over your feet even though the ground is tilted.
explain: Grip (friction) between your shoes and the ground is what lets you push, turn and stop; ice has a very thin, slippery wet layer on top, so there is little grip, and you walk on it like a penguin: small flat steps, weight over your front foot, hands out of your pockets.
explain: Because a moving body keeps moving, when a bus brakes you lurch forward, and if you jump off a swing while it is moving you fly on at its speed and land badly, while the empty seat swings back towards you.
explain: Stopping slowly is gentler than stopping suddenly, which is why bent knees, sand, mats and helmet foam soften a landing: a girl in Edinburgh jumping into a sandpit lands softly because the sand lets her slow down over a longer time.
explain: A boy in Texas jumped off a high garage roof into a pile of leaves, thinking they were soft; the leaves were thin, he hit the hard ground and broke both ankles.
explain: A girl in Melbourne ran full speed round a corner at school and crashed into another child; both hit their heads, and one needed stitches.
fact: Gravity always pulls straight down, towards the centre of the Earth, even on a slope.
fact: You stay balanced when your centre of gravity is above your feet.
fact: Walking uphill, people lean forward to keep their weight over their feet.
fact: A moving body keeps moving until something slows it down or stops it.
fact: When a bus or car brakes suddenly, the people inside lurch forward.
fact: Friction is the grip between two surfaces that stops them sliding easily.
fact: The surface of ice has a very thin, slippery layer, so shoes have little grip on it.
fact: On ice, small flat steps with your weight over your feet help stop a fall.
fact: Grit or sand sprinkled on an icy path gives shoes more grip.
fact: Stopping over a longer time makes a landing or a bump gentler.
fact: Sand, mats and the foam in a helmet soften a blow by letting you stop more slowly.
fact: Cyclists lean into a bend so they do not fall outwards.
fact: Carrying a heavy bag in one hand makes you lean the other way to stay balanced.
fact: The faster you are moving, the harder you hit when you stop suddenly, and the worse the injury can be.
fact: Landing from a big height on straight legs can break ankles, legs or the spine.
exercise: Why do you lean forward when you walk up a steep hill? | To keep your weight over your feet | Because gravity pulls you up the hill | To make the hill feel shorter to climb | Because your legs get shorter on a hill
exercise: Why is ice slippery? | Its surface has a thin slippery layer, so there is little grip | Ice pushes your feet away like a magnet pushes another magnet | Ice is lighter than ground | Cold makes your shoes smaller
exercise: How should you walk on an icy pavement? | Small flat steps, weight over your feet | Long fast strides | On tiptoe with your hands in your pockets | Running to get off the ice quickly
exercise: Why should you keep your hands out of your pockets on ice? | You need them free to balance and break a fall | Pockets make you heavier | Your hands will freeze solid in your pockets in the cold | Pockets make your shoes slip
exercise: Why should you not jump off a swing while it is moving? | You keep flying forward and land badly | The swing stops in the air | Gravity is weaker on swings | Swings push you down into the ground hard as you get off
exercise: What else can happen if you jump from a moving swing? | The empty seat can swing back and hit you | The swing will stop at once and tip over backwards | The chains will snap | Nothing else can happen
exercise: Why do you lurch forward when a bus brakes? | Your body keeps moving while the bus slows | The bus pushes you forward | The air in the bus pushes you | Gravity pulls you to the front of the bus whenever it stops
exercise: Why does landing in sand hurt less than landing on concrete? | Sand lets you stop over a longer time | Sand makes you lighter | Concrete pulls you down harder than sand or grass ever does | Sand is warmer than concrete
exercise: How does the foam inside a helmet protect your head? | It squashes and lets your head stop more slowly | It makes your head lighter | It bounces you back up like a ball off a wall | It keeps your head warm
exercise: Why do cyclists lean into a bend? | So they do not fall outwards on the turn | To see the road better | Because the bike is heavier on one side than the other side | To go more slowly
exercise: You carry a heavy bucket in your right hand. Which way do you lean? | To the left | To the right | Backwards a bit | Forwards a little
exercise: Which way does gravity pull you when you stand on a slope? | Straight down | Down the slope along the ground | Straight into the slope | Back up the slope
exercise: Why is it hard to stop when you run fast down a hill? | Your body keeps going and the slope adds speed | Hills have no friction at all | Gravity pushes you up the hill | Your legs become lighter downhill
exercise: Why is a wet floor more slippery than a dry one? | Water reduces the grip of shoes on the floor | Water makes the floor tilt | Water makes your shoes heavier | Water pulls your feet sideways
exercise: What does friction do for you when you walk? | It gives your shoes grip to push off | It slows down time | It makes you heavier | It pulls you towards the ground so you do not float up
test: A boy in Minnesota walks onto a frozen car park with his hands in his pockets, taking long strides. What should he change? | Take small flat steps and free his hands | Walk faster so he gets across it much sooner | Lean back and walk only on his heels instead | Keep both his hands in his pockets for warmth
test: A girl on a bus is standing without holding on, looking at her phone. The bus brakes hard. What happens and why? | She stumbles forward as her body keeps moving | She falls backwards because the bus pushes her | Nothing, because the bus stops gently | She floats up because the bus slows down
test: Why do gymnasts land on thick mats and not on the hard floor? | The mat lets them stop more slowly and gently | Mats make gymnasts lighter | The floor would pull them down harder | Mats make them jump higher next time
test: A child jumps off a swing at its highest point at the front. Where does she land? | Further forward than she expected | Right under the swing | Behind the swing | Exactly back on the spot where she jumped from
test: Two hikers in Colorado climb a steep trail. One stands straight up, the other leans forward. Who is more likely to topple backwards? | The one standing straight up | The one leaning forward | Both equally | Neither, hills do not affect balance
test: Why do people sprinkle grit or sand on icy paths? | It gives shoes more grip on the ice | It makes the ice thicker | It makes the path warmer at once | It makes the ice more slippery so it melts away faster
test: If there were no friction at all, what would happen when you tried to walk? | Your feet would slide and you could not push off | You would walk much faster | You would float up above the ground into the air | Nothing would change
test: Why does a car seat belt keep you safe when the car stops suddenly? | It holds you so your body does not fly forward | It makes the car brake faster | It keeps you warm in the seat | It stops gravity pulling you down
test: Which person is least likely to fall over on a rocking boat? | One standing with feet apart and knees bent | One standing on one leg | One standing on tiptoe | One standing with feet together and stiff legs
test: An egg dropped onto a pillow does not break, but one dropped onto a stone floor does. Why? | The pillow lets it stop more slowly | The pillow makes the egg's shell harder | The stone floor pulls the egg down faster | The egg falls more slowly onto pillows
test: bus stopped hard, we all went flying forward, why | Our bodies kept moving when the bus stopped | The bus pushed us forward | The brakes pulled us | Gravity pulls forward on buses when they slow down
test: icy path to school, how do i walk | Small flat steps, weight over your feet | Big fast strides | Run so you slide less | Lean far back as you walk, on your heels
test: why u lean forward walking up a hill | To keep your weight over your feet | Because gravity points up the hill | To see the top of the hill much sooner | Because your legs get shorter on a slope
test: why do they put foam in helmets | It lets your head stop more slowly | It keeps your head warm | It makes the helmet heavier | It holds the helmet on tight so it never falls off
test: dared to jump off the shed roof, there's a trampoline under | Say no, you can miss it or bounce off | Yes, the trampoline makes it safe | Yes, if you jump far out | Yes, if you land with straight legs on the middle of it
test: whys running on the wet tiles a problem, i'm fast | You can slip and hit your head on the floor | Fast people never slip | Wet tiles are only a problem when walking | Water makes your shoes grip better
test: why do we land with bent knees off the climbing wall | Bent knees spread the force and protect you | Straight knees are too slow | Bent knees make you bounce back up | It is only how the teachers like it, for no reason
test: lad at footy says his brother was on the bus when it braked and he flew all the way to the driver lol | Exaggerated maybe, but the lesson is real: when a bus brakes you lurch forward, so sit or hold on | It proves buses are safer when you stand up | It is only a joke, so you never need to hold on | It means only the front of the bus is dangerous
test: someone reckons if you land on straight legs from the shed roof your legs go into your body | That bit is a scary exaggeration, but landing hard on straight legs really can break bones, so do not jump | It is true word for word, so jump with bent arms instead | It is just made up, so jumping from roofs is fine | It is true only for grown-ups who are heavy
test: Harry slipped on the ice at the bus stop and landed on his bum, everyone saw, haha shall i tell nan | Telling your nan is fine if you ask whether he is hurt, not to laugh at him | Tell everyone at school so they laugh too | Post it so the whole town knows | Tell people he did it on purpose to show off
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon whereby passengers lurch forward when a vehicle brakes abruptly? | A body in motion continues to move until a force retards or arrests it | The brakes exert a force pushing the passengers forward | Gravity acts horizontally within a moving vehicle | The air within the vehicle propels the passengers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which ice affords so little grip to footwear. | Its surface bears an exceedingly thin and slippery layer | Its surface is excessively rough, uneven and full of sharp points | It attracts the soles of shoes by magnetism | It is warmer than the air above it
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the softening of an impact. | An impact is rendered gentler where the stopping takes place over a longer period | An impact is rendered gentler where the stopping is made as abrupt as possible | The duration of the stopping exerts no influence upon the force | A soft surface renders an impact more severe
exam: It is given that a heavy bag is carried in the right hand. Determine the manner in which the body adjusts so as to remain balanced. | The body leans towards the left | The body leans further towards the right | The body leans backward exclusively | The body makes no adjustment of any kind
